  "problem": "Expressed as a mixed number in its lowest terms, $1\\,\\frac{1}{7}\\times 1\\,\\frac{1}{6}=${{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we write $1\\,\\frac{1}{7}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{7}=\\frac{(1\\times 7)+1}{7}=\\frac{8}{7}\n$$\n\nWe also write $1\\,\\frac{1}{6}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{(1\\times 6)+1}{6}=\\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{7}\\times \\frac{7}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{7}\\times \\frac{7}{6} & =\\frac{8}{6}\\times \\frac{7}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{6}\\times 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we write this fraction in its lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{6}=\\frac{8÷2}{6÷2}=\\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3}=1\\,\\text{R}3=1\\,\\frac{1}{3}\n$$",
